+## Where the Money Gathers
+
+<!-- AI draft, 2026-11-17. Codex: established mechanisms moved out of Alice; preserves Fable's accumulation argument. -->
+A pile can fund what scattered savings cannot coordinate on their own: a railroad across a continent, a chip fabrication plant, the factory floor of Chapter 6. Banks, funds and companies gather claims held by many people and direct them toward a project. Gathering the capital need not mean giving it all to one person. Ownership, authority and the work being financed are three arrangements, even when one company bundles them together.
+
+The bundle can also buy power over the terms on which others work. Own the only usable port, a critical standard or an app store that others cannot readily bypass, and you can charge for passage. Centuries before the app store, English law prohibited buying up goods on their way to market to resell them dearer, under the splendid name of *forestalling*. Standard Oil later secured preferential railroad rates that helped squeeze competitors.[^v1-c7-chokepoints] Scale can lower costs; control of a bottleneck can raise everyone else's. A balance sheet does not announce which one paid the dividend.
+
+Purchasing power also changes what gets built and whom it serves. Markets register demand backed by the ability to pay, not need alone. Where desirable housing is scarce, richer bidders can push prices beyond other buyers' reach.[^v1-c7-positional] The distance between my closet slated for demolition and the estate down the shore was partly that. Assets can also secure loans: when collateral values rise, borrowing capacity can rise with them, financing further purchases. A fall can send the same machinery into reverse.[^v1-c7-collateral]
+
+Prices, then, can change the world they purport to describe. Rising prices invite purchases; those purchases support prices and collateral values, which enable more borrowing. Soros calls the interaction between beliefs and the conditions they help create *reflexivity*.[^v1-c7-soros] Minsky's financial-instability hypothesis adds that long calm can encourage financing arrangements increasingly dependent on refinancing or asset sales. A system that looked stable becomes vulnerable through the behavior its stability encouraged.[^v1-c7-minsky] These loops need no single rich puppeteer. Large positions and leverage can amplify them, but a crowd can supply the enthusiasm.
+
+Technology later made some advantages measurable in milliseconds. In 2010, a straighter fiber route from Chicago to the New Jersey exchange centers shortened transmission times; microwave networks subsequently beat the fiber. Traders paid to reach a price before someone else could update it.[^v1-c7-latency] That is speed, not clairvoyance. Learning adds another mechanism: in a 2020 study of simulated markets, pricing algorithms learned to sustain high prices through strategies that punished undercutting, without communicating with one another.[^v1-c7-algos] An algorithm can become very good at a market without making that market good for its customers.
