 ## Alice, Down the Rabbit Hole: The Weight of Money
 
 <!-- AI draft, 2026-11-17. -->
 <!-- Alice redrafted 2026-11-17 from Sam's premise (one-dimensional compression, accumulation, money-gravity in space and time, smart money). The forgetting-curve alternate is in chapter-7-alice-forgetting-2026-11-17.md; the original placeholder in chapter-7-before-2026-11-17.md. -->
-*The twist, in one line: money squeezes a society's many values into one number that forgets everything about a trade except the amount, and never forgets the amount, so it can pile up; and a large enough pile stops riding the invisible hand and starts bending it, now in time as well as in space.*
-
-Start with a strange claim. In 1998, an economic theorist showed that, in principle, money does the same job as memory.[^v1-c7-memory] Imagine a society with a perfect, shared record of every contribution anyone had ever made: every meal cooked, every road repaired, every favor done. In such a world, money would be unnecessary; you could simply consult the record to see who deserved what. Money exists because no such record exists. It's a portable, anonymous substitute for it: a token saying *I gave something of value, somewhere, to someone, and am owed something back*. Money is memory, carried in the pocket.
-
-But it's a memory with one dimension. Every trade is rich while it happens: who grew the apples, on whose land, with whose water, at what cost to the river below, with how much of the harvest owed to sunlight and soil that nobody paid for. When the trade is done, all of that is squeezed into a single number, and the rest is thrown away. Hayek saw why the squeeze works.[^v1-c7-hayek] The knowledge needed to run an economy (that a frost has struck the coffee harvest, that a port is on strike, that a new mine has opened) is scattered across millions of people, and a price gathers it anyway: when coffee becomes scarce, its price rises, and millions who know nothing about the frost drink a little less, exactly as if they knew. In the language of Interlude 2, a price is a lossy compression of the state of the world.
-
-For most of history, one dimension was the right design. Shells, coins, clay tablets and paper ledgers can carry a number; they can't carry a story. And the single number has a gift no richer record could match: it connects domains that share nothing else. You sell a house and buy apples with the same money, and neither the buyer of the house nor the grower of the apples needs to know the other exists. The common unit of the previous section, 999 prices instead of half a million, is that gift in arithmetic. But what the number drops doesn't stop happening. The river still pays; the oil the earth spent millions of years making is booked as if it had cost only the drilling. Economists call some of what falls out *externalities*, and some of it is simply windfall, value taken that nobody made.
-
-The squeeze has a second property, and it matters more. This memory forgets everything about a trade except the number, and it never forgets the number. A dollar is the same dollar whoever earned it and however; it doesn't fade, and it can be poured into any other dollar. So money, unlike bread or favors, can pile up in one place. The figure earlier in this chapter showed that the piles form even among equals following fair rules. Skill, inheritance and power only steepen them.
-
-Before asking what the piles do, notice what the invisible hand asks of the rest of us. It is an emergence, millions of small trades balancing supply against demand and laying out supply chains that no one designed, and money is the medium it emerges in. But the computing is done by people. Every shopper compares prices, reads reviews, tries the shoes, sends them back and writes a review for the next shopper: an unpaid tester for a market that can't sense anything by itself. Chapter 6's farmer domesticated the field and then had to report to it. The shopper domesticated the market, and reports to it daily, with the least time and attention to spare often belonging to the people with the least money. Which raises an obvious question. Why can't money do some of that work itself? We already poll, forecast and simulate demand before shortages arrive. A payment could carry more than one number: where the thing came from, what it cost the people and places along the way. Agents could do the comparing and haggling on our behalf. This isn't the central planner Hayek warned against, gathering everyone's knowledge in one office. The knowledge would travel with the money, as it does now in prices, only less compressed. Interlude 11 will show machines already representing meaning as long lists of numbers rather than one. Money could learn the same trick.
-
-But first, the piles. When people gather into crowds, Chapter 5 found, the crowd acquires properties none of its members has. Gather enough money in one place and the same thing happens. Some of what emerges is good. A pile can fund what no scattered savings could: a fleet to the Spice Islands, a railroad across a continent, a chip fabrication plant costing tens of billions, the factory floor of Chapter 6. Much of the useful work of large organizations exists because someone could gather a pile and point it at a goal. Some of what emerges is bad. A pile buys its holder the freedom to extract, to pollute, to undercut and buy out rivals, and it raises the bar for everyone else. When the richest bid for the houses with the best views, every house nearby gets dearer: the distance between my closet slated for demolition and the estate down the shore was partly that.[^v1-c7-positional]
-
-And some of what emerges is worse, because it turns the hand against its own purpose. Money was meant to make goods flow toward the people who need them most, at prices that tell the truth. A large enough pile can do the opposite. It can buy a chokepoint, the one railroad, the one port, the one app store, the one standard everyone must use, and tax every flow through it. It can divert flows toward itself, hoard what others need, and herd the crowd: Soros's reflexive loop, in which beliefs about the market change the market, which confirms the beliefs, and Minsky's warning that long calm breeds the confident borrowing that ends it, both run best with a heavy hand on the scale.[^v1-c7-soros][^v1-c7-minsky] None of this is new. Medieval England made it a crime to buy up goods on their way to market so as to resell them dearer, an offense with the splendid name of *forestalling*; a century and a half ago, an oil company squeezed its rivals by securing secret discounts from the railroads they all depended on.[^v1-c7-chokepoints]
-
-A useful picture is gravity. Interlude 1 found that information has a pull: the more that gathers in one place, the more it attracts. Money behaves the same way. A pile is mass in the invisible hand's spacetime. Prices still balance and goods still flow, but along curved paths, around the pile, and toward it; the rich get richer not by any rule written down but by the shape of the field.[^v1-c7-gravity] The hand still works. It works on a surface someone else has bent.
-
-Until recently, piles bent space: a lot of money in one place. Now they can bend time. The previous section ended on the economy's peculiar power, that the future can be spent before it arrives. Whoever sees that future first gets to spend it first. A fiber cable was laid on a nearly straight path from Chicago to the New York exchanges for traders who would pay to use it, and then chains of microwave towers were built to beat the cable, all to arrive a few thousandths of a second ahead of everyone else.[^v1-c7-latency] In laboratory markets, pricing algorithms left to learn on their own discovered how to keep prices high without ever communicating, punishing any rival that undercut them.[^v1-c7-algos] The owner of the best prediction is usually the owner of the biggest pile, and the prediction makes the pile bigger. It's Chapter 1's pattern in a new medium: a cheap expansion, this time of foresight, and a symmetry broken by who reaches it first.
-
-So here is where this rabbit hole leads. Past some weight, a market stops being an emergence that nobody controls and becomes an instrument that someone does: the invisible hand acquires a visible owner. The old counterweights (competition law, taxes that rise with fortune, open standards that keep chokepoints from forming) are attempts to flatten the field. The smart money imagined above cuts both ways. In the hands of the pile, prediction and agents bend time faster than any law can follow. Held by everyone, and carrying the memory that one-dimensional money throws away, they might make the curvature visible, and harder to hide.
-
-When the streets of Seattle filled in 1999, this was part of what the two sides were arguing about: whether the rules of world trade flattened the field for small countries and small producers, or let the heaviest players write the curve into law. They disagreed sharply about the answer. Neither disputed that whoever writes the rules shapes the field.
-
-If money is a memory with one dimension that piles into mass, what would a memory with many dimensions look like, one that could remember the river, the care and the forest, keep the cheapness that lets strangers coordinate, and refuse to let any pile bend the field it measures? That question belongs to Volume III.
